00:00:02.629 --> 00:00:07.589
Hi it's Michelle from lab muffin beauty
science: chemistry PhD and lover of

00:00:07.589 --> 00:00:12.360
retinoids. Retinoids are vitamin A
derivatives. They are some of the most

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.070
popular skincare ingredients and their
effectiveness is backed by a lot of

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:21.600
studies but like everything else in
skincare and beauty there are lots of myths

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:26.160
and half-truths about how they work and
how you're meant to use them so today

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:29.519
we're going to be talking about some of
these myths and we're going to bust them.

00:00:29.519 --> 00:00:32.790
If you like hearing about the science
behind beauty products you can give this

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:36.480
video a thumbs up, subscribe to my
channel, and click the notification bell

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.629
so you don't miss any videos. What is a
retinoid? Retinoids are ingredients

00:00:42.629 --> 00:00:46.800
that work like vitamin A they have lots
and lots of applications in skincare.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.750
Their main uses are to help with acne
and to help with aging; so things like

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:56.699
wrinkles and pigmentation. They're one of
the few things that can reverse the

00:00:56.699 --> 00:01:06.689
signs of aging. Some examples of
retinoids are retinol, tretinoin, adapalene, tazarotene, retinyl esters and retinaldehyde. Let's start with the

00:01:06.689 --> 00:01:12.600
first myth:
"Retinoids will thin your skin" This is

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.290
actually one of the myths that stopped
me from trying retinoids for a really

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:21.840
long time retinoids will thin one
specific layer of skin but overall they

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.490
will actually thicken it. They thicken
the living layer, so the dermis and the

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:32.159
epidermis above it, but they thin down or
compact the stratum corneum. So on my

00:01:32.159 --> 00:01:36.479
handi skin model they will thicken all
of these layers: the epidermis up here,

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:41.759
and the dermis down here, but they will
compact this layer: the stratum corneum

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:46.110
which is made up of dead cells. So the
way that retinol thickens the epidermis

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:50.909
is that it causes it to proliferate in
other words produce more cells

00:01:50.909 --> 00:01:54.840
retinoids also increase the production
of glycosaminoglycans

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:00.630
or GAG's. These are long, sugar-based
molecules that can hold on to lots of

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:05.009
water. They're like a humectant moisturizer
within your skin. One example is

00:02:05.009 --> 00:02:09.780
hyaluronic acid this means that with
this extra water retention it plumps up

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:13.770
your skin. Retinoids also prevent
collagen degradation and increase its

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:16.150
production.
And so that means that you will get a

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:20.110
thicker dermis. Retinoids can also
compact the stratum corneum which is

00:02:20.110 --> 00:02:23.799
generally a good thing when you have a
thinner stratum corneum this is the dead

00:02:23.799 --> 00:02:28.720
cells on the surface of the skin this
actually makes your skin look glowier and

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:32.950
smoother. But thinning the stratum
corneum can also have negative effects.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.970
The stratum corneum is there to protect
the living layers of your skin and so if

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.480
it gets too thin it can lead to
sensitive skin. The thickening of the

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.650
lower layers is actually what helps give
retinoids their anti-aging effect. If you

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:50.170
have plumper skin then it looks smoother
and wrinkles are less deep. "Retinoids

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:54.060
are light sensitive so you need to use
them in the dark"

00:02:54.060 --> 00:03:00.459
This is partly true; a lot of retinoid
ingredients are unstable in light and so

00:03:00.459 --> 00:03:04.239
if you have too much light exposure they
break down, which means they will no

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:08.170
longer work this is especially the case
with sunlight. Sunlight is one of the

00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:12.310
most intense sources of light that we
get but there are some retinoids that

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.360
aren't light-sensitive so for example
some of the newer ones like adapalene

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.870
and tazarotene. They aren't light
sensitive so you can actually use them

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.069
during the day and it says this on the
packet. Tretinoin is generally light

00:03:24.069 --> 00:03:28.510
sensitive but it can be formulated so
that it isn't. Micronized tretinoin

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:32.440
for example, is photo stable which means
that it is not sensitive to light

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:36.670
exposure. Retinol is generally unstable
and so it breaks down in light, which

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:40.120
means that you should be applying it at
night. If you're not sure whether your

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.019
particular retinoid product is photo
stable, look at the packaging and do

00:03:44.019 --> 00:03:48.940
whatever the instructions tell you to do.
"If you don't see immediate results with

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:54.490
your retinoid, you should give up"
This is not true for skincare products

00:03:54.490 --> 00:03:58.900
in general, and it is especially not true
for retinoids. Some people will see

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:03.490
immediate results but a lot of people
will not and that doesn't mean that the

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:06.760
retinoid isn't working. Unlike a lot of
other skincare products

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.900
retinoids can have effects deeper in the
skin, and to get there and have these

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.340
effects, it can take a bit longer so you
should be using retinoids for a longer

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:19.269
time before dismissing them as something
that doesn't work for you. They work on a

00:04:19.269 --> 00:04:23.350
longer timeframe than most other
ingredients so a good rule of thumb is

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:28.090
give it three to six months before you
decide it's not for you.

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:33.820
"Retinoids can't give immediate results" On the flipside, some people say that retinoids

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:37.990
cannot possibly give you immediate
results and anything that you see early

00:04:37.990 --> 00:04:41.350
on is either just in your head or
because of some other products that

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:45.250
you're using. But retinoids have been
documented to give some people very

00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:49.840
quick results in peer-reviewed studies.
The fastest results are that your skin

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:54.280
might look smoother and you get a pink
or rosy glow. But note that this doesn't

00:04:54.280 --> 00:04:58.630
happen with everyone which i think is
one of the reasons this myth exists. Some

00:04:58.630 --> 00:05:02.470
other reasons for this myth is that most
trials on retinoids lasts longer but

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.890
just because they didn't measure the
results earlier doesn't mean that they

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:09.640
didn't exist. There's also the fact that
skin turnover tends to take a while

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:13.870
and so some results won't be immediately
visible but there are some effects of

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:18.100
retinoids that don't require skin
turnover to see. This is especially the

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.360
changes that take place on the shallower
layers of your skin, so for example, the

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.870
compaction of the stratum corneum
happens quite quickly. The changes in the

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:30.580
upper epidermis also are very fast.
Results that require changes deeper in

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:34.180
the skin so things like reduction of
deep wrinkles, or reduction of

00:05:34.180 --> 00:05:38.500
pigmentation these will take a bit
longer so again these early results

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:41.650
don't happen for everyone and if you
don't have early results it doesn't

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:45.730
necessarily mean that you won't have
results later on. In one study with

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:50.290
tretinoin, five to ten percent of people
saw changes within two weeks. About half

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:54.070
of the people saw changes within a month, and almost everyone had changes within

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:58.030
12 weeks.
"Don't use retinoids until you see signs

00:05:58.030 --> 00:06:04.840
of aging" This myth is not true for a few
reasons. There is no evidence that using

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:08.060
retinoids early or 
using them long-term will cause any sort

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:12.260
of skin damage. The changes in your skin
that lead to skin aging will occur

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:17.180
before you can actually see any signs of
aging on your skin, so retinoids can stop

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:20.810
and help reverse these microscopic
signs before they start showing up.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:25.070
Retinoids are also useful for things
other than anti-aging. The other thing

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:29.389
that retinoids are good for is acne
resinize help with skin turnover and it

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:33.440
reduces the tiny microscopic clogs or
microcomedones that happen in your

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:37.340
skin before you start getting pimples.
You also won't run out of skin because

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:41.570
your skin is turning over too fast, this
is not how skin works. So there is no

00:06:41.570 --> 00:06:46.220
limit on how old you have to be before
you can start using retinoids.

00:06:46.220 --> 00:06:50.600
"Bukuchiol is better than retinol" I've
actually even seen someone say that the

00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.530
bakuchiol is better than prescription
tretinoin, which is classified as a drug.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:59.479
Bakuchiol is an ingredient that's found
in the bad Chi plant. There's been a lot

00:06:59.479 --> 00:07:02.780
of hype about how it is a natural
retinol, or that it's better than

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:07.550
retinol, or it's just as good as retinol
without any of the downsides. This is

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:12.139
pretty far-fetched. There's been one
recent independent clinical study that's

00:07:12.139 --> 00:07:16.370
open access which is pretty impressive.
It was a 12-week trial where 44 people

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:20.990
applied either 0.5%
of retinol once a day or 0.5%

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:24.860
bakuchiol twice a day on their skin.
At the end of the study they found that

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:29.060
bakuchiol and retinol reduce wrinkles and
hyperpigmentation around the same amount

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:33.470
but people using retinol have more
peeling and stinging, while people use in

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:37.160
Bakuchiol have more redness. The hype
also partly comes from the fact that

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:41.510
Bakuchiol and retinol have some similarities in how they work on a microscopic level

00:07:41.510 --> 00:07:45.710
but the big difference is firstly bakuchiol
had to be used twice a day whereas

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.639
retinol was used once a day. There's also
nowhere near the same amount of evidence

00:07:49.639 --> 00:07:54.950
for bakuchiol compared to retinoids. So
bakuchiol is a promising ingredient but

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:59.240
it doesn't really belong in the
conversation about retinoids.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.270
"You need to use retinoids daily to have any effect"

00:08:03.270 --> 00:08:07.949
This is not true, there's been studies
where tretinoin was used once a week

00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:11.880
or three times a week or every second
day and they found that there were

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:15.750
improvements in skin. Most of these
studies were done on tretinoin, which is

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:18.504
a particularly strong, prescription-only
retinoid, and this is the case with most

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:24.030
studies on retinoids: there's lots of
data and tretinoin, but not much on

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:27.509
other retinoids. But there's no reason
why this doesn't apply to other retinoids

00:08:27.509 --> 00:08:32.310
as well. So these are the top myths on
retinoids. If you have any other myths

00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:35.880
that you've been wondering about leave
them in the comments. If you want to get

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:39.149
started on retinoids you can check
out my video on how to introduce them

00:08:39.149 --> 00:08:42.419
slowly. You'll also want to have a gentle
cleanser and a moisturizer and a

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:45.779
sunscreen already in your routine for
more on this you can check out my ebook

